      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We are given that \\( RSP \\) is a straight line and \\( \\angle QSP = 80^\\circ \\). We need to find the measure of \\( \\angle PQR \\).\n\nFirst, let's determine the measure of \\( \\angle SQR \\). Since \\( RSP \\) is a straight line, the sum of the angles around point \\( Q \\) is \\( 180^\\circ \\). Therefore, we can write the equation for the sum of the angles around point \\( Q \\) as:\n\\[\n\\angle QSP + \\angle SQR + \\angle RQ S = 180^\\circ\n\\]\nWe know \\( \\angle QSP = 80^\\circ \\) and the total angle around point \\( Q \\) is \\( 180^\\circ \\). Let \\( \\angle SQR = x \\). Then we have:\n\\[\n80^\\circ + x + \\angle RQS = 180^\\circ\n\\]\nSince \\( \\angle RQS \\) is part of \\( \\angle SQR \\), and we don't need its specific measure to find \\( \\angle PQR \\), we can simplify the equation to:\n\\[\nx + \\angle RQS = 100^\\circ\n\\]\nNow, we need to find \\( \\angle PQR \\). Notice that \\( \\angle PQR \\) is part of \\( \\angle QSP \\). Since \\( QSP \\) is a straight line, \\( \\angle PQR \\) is the supplement of \\( \\angle SQR \\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\angle PQR = 180^\\circ - \\angle SQR\n\\]\nFrom the equation \\( x + \\angle RQS = 100^\\circ \\), we can express \\( \\angle SQR \\) as:\n\\[\nx = 100^\\circ - \\angle RQS\n\\]\nSince \\( \\angle PQR \\) is the supplement of \\( \\angle SQR \\), we have:\n\\[\n\\angle PQR = 180^\\circ - x\n\\]\nSubstituting \\( x = 100^\\circ - \\angle RQS \\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\angle PQR = 180^\\circ - (100^\\circ - \\angle RQS) = 80^\\circ + \\angle RQS\n\\]\nSince \\( \\angle RQS \\) is part of \\( \\angle SQR \\) and we don't need its specific measure, we can directly use the fact that \\( \\angle PQR \\) is the supplement of \\( \\angle SQR \\), which is \\( 80^\\circ \\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\angle PQR = 180^\\circ - 80^\\circ = 100^\\circ - \\angle RQS = 100^\\circ - 100^\\circ = 80^\\circ\n\\]\nThus, the measure of \\( \\angle PQR \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{100}\n\\]",
